provider_responses.text:
The most important point is: **do not accept a verbal dismissal—get everything in writing.**

In Portugal, an employer should not simply tell you, “Don’t come tomorrow,” and treat that as the end of your job. The type of termination matters a lot for your rights, final pay, possible compensation, and unemployment registration.

Ask for a written document stating:

- your employer’s name and company details;
- your name and job;
- the date your employment ends;
- the reason the contract is ending;
- whether it is dismissal, end of a fixed-term contract, mutual agreement, or something else;
- information/documents needed for Social Security and IEFP;
- the calculation of money they will pay you.

Do not sign anything you do not understand, especially a document saying that you **resigned voluntarily** (“denúncia do contrato” / “rescisão por iniciativa do trabalhador”). If you sign a resignation when you did not actually choose to resign, it can make it much harder to claim unemployment support or challenge the situation later.

Also keep evidence:

- your contract and any amendments;
- payslips;
- work schedules/timesheets;
- WhatsApp messages, emails, and recordings of instructions where legal;
- proof of payment into your bank;
- the termination letter and final settlement document.

If they tell you verbally not to return, send a calm written message the same day, for example:

> “Following our conversation today, please confirm in writing whether my employment contract has been terminated, the reason for termination, and my final working day. I remain available to work according to my contract until I receive formal instructions.”

This creates evidence that **you did not abandon the job** or resign. Keep going to work as scheduled unless they clearly instruct you in writing not to, or it is unsafe to do so.

If the employer refuses to provide documents, delays pay, pressures you to sign, or says you are fired without explanation, contact **ACT** (Autoridade para as Condições do Trabalho) or a Portuguese labour lawyer quickly.

